          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Category of Sets
            Has
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+object"} Enough Projectives
            %strong CoSHEP
            (pronounced /ko:-shep/). This is more commonly known as the
            = succeed ":" do
              %strong presentation axiom
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
          %h2#Statement Statement
          %p In elementary terms, CoSHEP states
          .num_defn
            %h6#axiom_coshep Axiom (CoSHEP)
            %p
              For every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              and a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjection"} surjection
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ↠
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \twoheadrightarrow P
              has a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              The full axiom of choice states that every surjection
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to A
              has a section; hence in the above
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              may be chosen to be
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              itself.
          %p
            This should be read in view of the definition of
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+objects"} projective objects
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              An
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              in a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is (externally)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+object"} projective
              iff the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-functor"} hom-functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(P, -): C \to Set
              takes
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epis
              to epis. This is the same as saying: given an epi
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p: B \to A
              and a map
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi P
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: P \to A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi P
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g: P \to B
              in the sense that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo =
                      %mi p
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = p \circ g
          %p
            Accordingly, in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            the CoSHEP axiom says equivalently
          .num_defn
            %h6#axiom_coshep_2 Axiom (CoSHEP)
            %p
              Every object has a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+presentation"} projective presentation
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "projective+object#EnoughProjectives"} enough projectives
          %p
            Borrowing from the philosophy of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructivism
            = succeed "." do
              %em complete presentation
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} dual
              axiom, that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
              has
              %a{:href => "injective+object#EnoughInjectives"} enough injectives
              (that is, every set admits an injection into an injective set) is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/true"} true
              in every
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+object"} power object
              is an
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+object"} injective object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              embeds in its power object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X
              via the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singleton+subset"} singleton
              map
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\cdot\}:X\hookrightarrow P X
          %h2#consequences Consequences
          %p
            The existence of sufficiently many
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+presentations"} projective presentations
            plays a central role in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            as a means to construct
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+resolutions"} projective resolutions
            of objects. Tradtionally one often uses the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            to prove that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+modules"} categories of modules
            have
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "projective+object#EnoughProjectives"} enough projectives
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+modules"} free modules
            are projective.
          %p
            But the weaker assumption of CoSHEP is already sufficient for this purpose: while not every free module will be projective, one can still use CoSHEP to find a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+presentation"} projective presentation
            for every free module (and thus for every module). This is discussed in more detail
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "projective+object#EnoughWithCOSHEP"} here
          #ImpliesDependentAndCountableChoice.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              The following three conditions on a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/W-pretopos"} W-pretopos
              with
              %a{:href => "projective+object#EnoughProjectives"} enough projectives
              are equivalent:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  The axiom of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+choice"} dependent choice
                  (DC),
              %li
                %p
                  The axiom of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+choice"} countable choice
                  (CC),
              %li
                %p
                  Projectivity of the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singleton"} singleton
                  (the
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
          %p
            Note that we normally assume (3) for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/the+category+of+sets"} the category of sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-pointed+pretopos"} well-pointed pretopos
            and true
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internally
            in any pretopos whatsoever, so one normally says that DC and CC simply follow from the existence of enough projectives (CoSHEP). Equivalently, internal DC and internal CC follow from internal CoSHEP.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Condition 1 easily implies 2. Condition 2 says precisely that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
              is externally projective, and since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              is a retract of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be inhabited, so there exists an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entire+relation"} entire relation
              given by a jointly monic span
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo ←
                      %mi epi
                    %mi U
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi f
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \stackrel{epi}{\leftarrow} U \stackrel{f}{\to} X,
            %p and similarly let
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo ←
                      %mrow
                        %mi epi
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 1
                    %mi R
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 2
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \stackrel{epi \pi_1}{\leftarrow} R \stackrel{\pi_2}{\to} X
            %p
              be an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entire+relation"} entire binary relation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi P
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p: P \to X
              be a projective cover. Since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              is assumed projective, the cover
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \to 1
              admits a section
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                      %mo :
                      %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma: 1 \to U
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mi σ
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \sigma: 1 \to X
              lifts through
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              to an element
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 0
                      %mo :
                      %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_0: 1 \to P
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              lifts through the epi
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1
              to a map
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi q
                      %mo :
                      %mi P
                      %mo →
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q: P \to R
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 2
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_2 q
              lifts through
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              to a map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
              (since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              is projective):
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi P
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi ϕ
                          %mtd
                            %mi P
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↙
                            %mi p
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi q
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi p
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ←
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi π
                                  %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mi R
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi π
                                  %mn 2
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp; &amp; P  &amp; \stackrel{\phi}{\to} &amp; P \\
                    \&amp; \swarrow p &amp; \downarrow q &amp; &amp; \downarrow p \\
                    X &amp; \underset{\pi_1}{\leftarrow} &amp; R &amp; \underset{\pi_2}{\to} &amp; X
                    }
            %p
              By the universal property of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
              (see
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/recursion"} recursion
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h: \mathbb{N} \to P
              rendering commutative the diagram
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mi ℕ
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi s
                          %mtd
                            %mi ℕ
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi id
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi h
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi h
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi x
                                  %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mi P
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mi ϕ
                          %mtd
                            %mi P
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↙
                            %mi p
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi q
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi p
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ←
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi π
                                  %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mi R
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi π
                                  %mn 2
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    1 &amp; \stackrel{0}{\to} &amp; \mathbb{N} &amp; \stackrel{s}{\to} &amp; \mathbb{N} \\
                    id \downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow h &amp; &amp; \downarrow h \\
                    1 &amp; \underset{x_0}{\to} &amp; P &amp; \underset{\phi}{\to} &amp; P \\
                    \&amp; \swarrow p &amp; \downarrow q &amp; &amp; \downarrow p \\
                    X &amp; \underset{\pi_1}{\leftarrow} &amp; R &amp; \underset{\pi_2}{\to} &amp; X
                    }
            %p
              Clearly
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi p
                    %mi h
                    %mo ,
                    %mi p
                    %mi h
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle p h, p h s \rangle : \mathbb{N} \to X \times X
              factors through
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mo :
                      %mi R
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \pi_1, \pi_2 \rangle : R \to X \times X
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∀
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo :
                          %mi ℕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi p
                      %mi h
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi p
                      %mi h
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_{n: \mathbb{N}} (p h(n), p h(n+1)) \in R
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              A topos in which CoSHEP holds but
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              is not projective is
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Set
                        %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is the category with three objects and exactly two non-identity arrows
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo →
                      %mi b
                      %mo ←
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \to b \leftarrow c
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U: C \to Set
              is a functor with
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(a) = \{a_0\}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi b
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi b
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(b) = \{b_0, b_1\}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(c) = \{c_0\}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo →
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(a \to b)(a_0) = b_0
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo →
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi b
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(c \to b)(c_0) = b_1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \to 1
              is epi but has no section, so
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              is not projective. On the other hand, as noted below, every presheaf topos satisfies CoSHEP, assuming that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
              itself does.
          %p
            CoSHEP also implies several weaker forms of choice, such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+multiple+choice"} axiom of multiple choice
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WISC"} WISC
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} predicative mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+sets"} function sets
            to show the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset+collection"} subset collection
            axiom.
          %h2#Variants Variants of the presentation axiom
          %h3#split_surjections  Split surjections
          %p
            In the absence of the full
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjections"} surjections
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+surjections"} split surjections
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                For every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                and a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjection"} surjection
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mo →
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to A
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ↠
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \twoheadrightarrow P
                has a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            %li
              %p
                For every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                and a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+surjection"} split surjection
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mo →
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to A
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ↠
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \twoheadrightarrow P
                has a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            %li
              %p
                For every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                and a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjection"} surjection
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mo →
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to A
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ↠
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \twoheadrightarrow P
                has a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            %li
              %p
                For every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                and a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+surjection"} split surjection
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mo →
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to A
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ↠
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \twoheadrightarrow P
                has a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
          %p By definition of split surjection, every split surjection has a section, so the third and fourth versions of the presentation axioms are simply:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                For every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                and a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjection"} surjection
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mo →
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to A
            %li
              %p
                For every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                and a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+surjection"} split surjection
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mo →
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to A
          %p
            These two versions of the presentation axiom are always true: take the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            to be
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and the (split) surjection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to A
            to be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+function"} identity function
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            This leaves the first and second version of the presentation axiom as non-trivial axioms that one can add to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is stronger than the version using a surjection into
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            holds. And then the usual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            is simply the presentation axiom where the surjection is required to be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijection
          %h3#external_and_internal_versions External and internal versions
          %p
            In addition, every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            has an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            defined on its
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsingletons"} subsingletons
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsingleton"} subsingleton
                represents a proposition
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty set
                represents
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/falsehood"} falsehood
            %li
              %p
                Any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singleton"} singleton
                represents
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth"} truth
            %li
              %p
                The binary cartesian product of two subsingletons is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjunction"} conjunction
                of propositions
            %li
              %p
                The function set between two subsingletons is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/implication"} implication
                of propositions
            %li
              %p
                The function set from a subsingleton to the empty set is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negation"} negation
                of propositions
            %li
              %p
                Given a family of subsingletons, the indexed
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
                of the family of subsingletons is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantification"} universal quantification
            %li
              %p
                One can turn any set into a subsingleton by taking the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
                of the unique function into a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singleton"} singleton
            %li
              %p
                The image of the unique function from the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
                of two subsingletons to any singleton is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjunction"} disjunction
                of propositions
            %li
              %p
                The image of the unique function from the indexed
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
                of a family of subsingletons to any singleton is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/existential+quantifier"} existential quantifier
            %li
              %p
                Equality is given by the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal+subset"} diagonal subset
          %p Then there also exist internal versions of the presentation axiom, which states that the presentation axiom is true when expressed internally in the set theory, that a particular subsingleton defined using the set theoretic operations above is a singleton.
          %h3#bhk_interpretation_of_the_presentation_axiom BHK interpretation of the presentation axiom
          %p In addition, there are two different ways to interpret predicate logic in the internal logic of a set theory:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                There is the traditional way of interpreting the internal logic, which takes
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+subsingletons"} propositions as subsingletons
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjunction"} disjunction
                for “or” and the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/existential+quantifier"} existential quantifier
                for “there exists, suitably defined in the internal logic.
            %li
              %p
                There is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BHK+interpretation"} BHK interpretation
                of the internal logic, which takes
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+sets"} propositions as sets
                and directly uses binary
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+unions"} disjoint unions
                for “or” instead of the disjunction, as well as indexed disjoint unions for “there exist” instead of the existential quantifier.
          %p This means that we get even more versions of the presentation axiom in set theory, depending on where one uses the traditional interpretation of predicate logic and where one uses the BHK interpretation of predicate logic in the internal logic:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                For surjections, whether the fibers of the function
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi P
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:P \to A
                are internally inhabited or pointed
            %li
              %p
                For split surjections, whether given a function
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi P
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:P \to A
                there exists a section
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                        %mo →
                        %mi P
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:A \to P
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi P
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ×
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f, g):(P \to A) \times (A \to P)
          %p All of these combine together to form a huge number of possible axioms of the presentation axiom, ranging from the provable to stronger than the usual presentation axiom.
          %h3#in_dependent_type_theory In dependent type theory
          %p In dependent type theory, not all types are sets, and sets (and other types) are usually elements of types called universes, so there are even more versions of the presentation axiom, depending on
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Whether given a set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo :
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A:U
                there exists a set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mo :
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P:U
                or whether one has a pair of sets
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi P
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo :
                        %mi U
                        %mo ×
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A, P):U \times U
            %li
              %p Whether one uses sets or types in defining the presentation axiom.
          %h2#topos In a topos
          %p
            When working in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            = succeed ".\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internally+projective+object"} internally projective object
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              Suppose that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              is (externally) projective in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              satisfies PAx whenever it satisfies internal PAx.
          %p
            Internal PAx does not follows from external PAx; see Counterexample 5.3. However, if
            %em every
            object is projective (AC), then every object is internally projective (IAC).
          %p
            A stronger version of PAx may be worth considering. Say that an object is
            %strong stably projective
            if its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            to any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+category"} slice category
            is projective. Then stably projective objects are internally projective (proof?). Similarly, if we say that a topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            satisfies stable PAx if every object is covered by a stably projective object, then a topos satisfies internal PAx if it satisfies stable PAx.
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf+topos"} presheaf topos
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{C^{op}}
              has enough projectives, since any coproduct of representables is projective. If in addition
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              has binary products, then by
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "/nlab/show/internally+projective+object#presheaf"} this result
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{C^{op}}
              validates internal PAx.
          #counter.num_example
            %h6#counterexample Counterexample
            %p
              However, not every presheaf topos validates internal PAx, even though every presheaf topos validates external PAx. As an example, let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              be the category where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ob
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ob(C)
              is the disjoint sum
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
